<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drangiana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zarangiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drangiana was an ancient region in the basin of the Helmand river and Lake Hamun, on the borders of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and was crossed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 330 BCE. After Saka groups settled there in the late second century BCE it became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a>, the origin of the modern name Sistan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drangiana.jpg" alt="Drangiana" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mossmaps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Satrapies_of_the_Achaemenid_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Drangiana, also called Zarangiana, was a historical region and an administrative division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. It lay around Lake Hamun, in the Sistan Basin on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> border, and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmand-river">Helmand River</a>, its main watershed. In ancient times an Iranian tribe lived there that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called Sarangians or Drangians. Strabo noted that they resembled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in their way of life, except that they had little wine and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> was found in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> possibly subdued Drangiana, and the Persian Achaemenid Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> certainly did. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> the Drangians were placed in the same district as the Utians, Thamanaeans, Mycians and those deported to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Its capital, Zarin or Zranka, is identified with great probability with the extensive Achaemenid site of Dahan-e Gholaman, southeast of Zabol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Another centre was Prophthasia, possibly at modern Farah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. At times the same <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> governed Drangiana and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered the region in 330–329 BCE, and it remained an administrative district under him and his successors. At his death in 323 BCE it was governed by Stasanor of Soloi, and in 321 BCE it was allotted to another Cypriot, Stasandros. By the end of the 4th century BCE it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, but in the later 3rd century BCE Euthydemos I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> at least temporarily annexed it. Antiochos III seems to have recovered it for the Seleucids in 206–205 BCE. Its history as Seleucid rule weakened is unclear, and by the mid-2nd century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> of the Arsacids had conquered it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drangiana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their displacement in the second century BCE, Saka groups moved into Bactria, into <b>Drangiana</b>, which took the name Sakastan (modern Sistan), and through the mountains into Gandhara and north-western India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
